.TH CLOG_OPEN_FILE_MP 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
clog_open_file_mp, clog_open_file \- create a file-backed logger with optional log rotation
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/clogger.h>

clog clog_open_file_mp(const char *path, clog_level_t min_level,
                    const clog_rotation_cfg_t *cfg,
                    const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg,
                    ccol_memmgmt_procs_t *mprocs);
/* convenience wrapper; default allocator */
static inline clog clog_open_file(const char *path,
                    clog_level_t min_level,
                    const clog_rotation_cfg_t *cfg,
                    const clog_async_cfg_t *async_cfg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a new logger that writes to the file at path.  When the file
does not exist, the function creates it with mode 0644, which the
umask of the process narrows; when it does exist, the function opens
it for append.  For a file that already exists, the function finds the
current size, asking fstat(2) first and falling back to an lseek(2) to
the end of the file, so that the thresholds for size rotation are
correct for an append to a log that already exists.  When the function
can find that size by neither method and cfg->size_rotation_enabled is
true, the call fails and returns CLOG_INVALID instead of silently
starting the byte counter from zero, which would let the file grow
well past cfg->max_file_size before its first rotation.

Log rotation is on only when cfg is not NULL.  You can turn size
rotation and time rotation on separately.

When rotation is on, the logger opens the directory of path and keeps
it open for its whole life.  It opens the file, and does every rename,
listing, compression and deletion of a rotation, relative to that
directory, so a relative path keeps naming the same file after the
process changes its working directory with chdir(2).

Size rotation triggers after the write that takes the total bytes
written to cfg->max_file_size or past it; the library checks it after
each write.

Time rotation triggers on the first write after
cfg->rotation_interval_us microseconds pass.  The check runs on whole
seconds, so an interval that is not a whole number of seconds rotates
at the first whole second at or after its end.  That time counts from
the last rotation.  Before the first rotation of the logger, when the
file already holds data, it counts from the moment at which that file
began: the stamp of the newest rotated generation on disk, because the
rotation that made it also created the current file. Without such a
generation, it is the birth time of the file, where the system reports
one (statx(2) on Linux, and stat(2) on FreeBSD and macOS), or otherwise
the last modification time.  On FreeBSD, a change of the modification
time to an earlier moment also moves the birth time to that
moment.  When the file is empty or new, the interval counts from the
creation of the logger, so a process that runs for less than the
interval, or restarts often, still rotates.  A starting moment in the
future counts as the creation of the logger.  The library checks the
interval before each write.

When a rotation fires, the library first creates the next live file
under a private name, <path>.tmp followed by 11 random hex digits,
with O_CREAT, O_EXCL and O_NOFOLLOW, so the file is always a new
regular file that the library made.  It then renames the current file
to <path>.<YYYYMMDDHHMMSS>, with a rename that never replaces an entry
that already has that name, and renames the new file onto <path>.
Both renames are atomic, and the rotation never opens <path> or a
rotated name.  A link, a FIFO or a file that another user puts at
<path> while a rotation runs is replaced by the new live file: it
never receives a record and never blocks the rotation.  A process that
stops between the creation of the private file and its rename leaves
that file behind empty; the next rotating open of path removes such an
empty regular file, and nothing else at a name of that shape.  A
second rotation inside the same second takes a collision suffix, which
runs _0001, _0002 and so on; the suffix is zero-padded to four digits,
and a number above 9999 takes the digits that it needs, such as
_10000.  Every rotation takes a name above every rotated name before
it: a later rotation inside the same second takes the next number, and
never a name that a deletion freed.  A logger that starts in a
directory which already holds rotated generations (see below) numbers
its rotations above the newest of them, so a process that restarts
inside the same second continues the sequence.  When the clock reads
an earlier second than the newest rotated name, the rotation keeps the
stamp of that name and takes the next number.  As logrotate(8) does,
the new live file gets the permission bits of the file that it
replaces, whatever the umask, and the owner and the group of that file
where the process may give them.  When the process cannot give it the
group, the library clears the group bits, so that no other group gains
access.  A log that its operator restricted, to 0600 or 0640 for
example, therefore stays restricted in every generation, and each
\&.gz file takes the bits of its source.  Once the new live file holds
<path>, the library deletes the oldest rotated files past
cfg->max_rotated_files.  A rotation that fails never deletes a rotated
file on its own account.

A directory entry is a rotated generation of the log only when its
name matches <path>.<YYYYMMDDHHMMSS>[_N][.gz] exactly, where N is four
zero-padded digits or a number from 10000 to 999999999, and the entry,
examined without following a link, is a regular file whose owner is
the effective user of the process or the owner of the live file, and
whose timestamp lies no more than 30 days above both the clock and the
newest name that the logger made.  The naming of rotations, the
deletion, the compression and the recovery of compressions act on
generations alone.  Every other entry is never opened, compressed,
renamed or deleted, and takes no place among the
cfg->max_rotated_files kept.  That covers an unrelated file whose name
starts with a timestamp of a similar shape, a link, a FIFO, a file
that another user owns, and a name stamped far in the future, so
another user who can write the directory cannot make the logger
write, read, archive or delete a file through a link, block on a
FIFO, or stop its rotations.  An entry that matches the name but is
not a generation gets a "log rotation: ignored an entry that is not a
rotated file of this log: <name>" record at CLOG_WARN in the live
file, with every character outside printable ASCII, every quote and
every backslash written as '?'.  At most 8 such records go out for a
log in any minute.

When path itself is a symbolic link, the logger writes through the
link until the first rotation, which moves the link to a rotated name.
From then on the live file is a regular file that the logger made, and
the link, which is not a generation, stays where the rotation put it.

The timestamp and the collision number of each rotated name decide
which rotated files are the oldest: the library orders the names by
the timestamp and then by the number, as a number.  Because every
rotation takes a name above every name before it, that order is the
order of the rotations, and the deletion always keeps the newest
generations.
The library does not use the modification time of a rotated file,
because the compression step writes a compressed rotated file, so its
modification time records when that compression finished and not how
old the log content inside it is.

The prune pass counts generations, where a generation is one rotation:
the uncompressed file and the \&.gz file of one generation count once.
The prune pass of a rotation never deletes the generation that the
same rotation has just created, and still counts it against
cfg->max_rotated_files.  A prune pass never deletes the generation
whose compression is running, and still counts it.  A pass keeps the
newest cfg->max_rotated_files generations and deletes every older one,
except an older one whose compression is running, which the library
deletes as soon as that compression ends.  A generation that still
waits for its compression is deleted like any other once it is older
than the newest cfg->max_rotated_files.  So at most
cfg->max_rotated_files generations plus the one under compression stay
on the disk.

When cfg->compress_rotated is true, zlib gzip-compresses each rotated
file after the rotation.  The library writes the compressed file to
<rotated-name>.gz and, after a success, removes the original
uncompressed file.  The work is done by a compression thread of the
logger, which the first rotation that needs it starts, and which
compresses one file at a time, the oldest file first.

The rotation only puts the file in a queue, so the thread that logs,
the async writer thread, and a producer behind a bounded async queue
never wait for gzip.  The last clog_close(3) of the file waits until
all queued compressions are complete, and a clog_fatal(3) record
waits for them within the budget of the exit drain below.

When the directory cannot hold the name <rotated-name>.gz.tmp
(NAME_MAX, 255 bytes on common filesystems), no compression of that
file can succeed: the file stays uncompressed, and the library writes
a "log rotation: a rotated file stays uncompressed: ..." record at
CLOG_WARN into the new live file.

The standard gunzip(1) and zcat(1) tools can read the \&.gz files that
the library makes.  A program that links the static library must also
link with -lz, which pkg-config --static --libs ccollections adds.
The shared library has zlib as its own dependency.

A compression opens its source with O_NOFOLLOW and O_NONBLOCK, and
reads it only when the descriptor is a regular file that the effective
user or the owner of the live file owns, so it never reads through a
link and never waits on a FIFO or a device.
A compression writes its output to <rotated-name>.gz.tmp, flushes it
to the disk, and only then gives it the name <rotated-name>.gz and
removes the uncompressed file, so a \&.gz file always holds a complete
gzip stream, even when the process stops in the middle of a
compression.  The prune pass and the naming of rotations never look
at the \&.tmp file.  A filesystem whose fsync(2) answers EINVAL or
ENOSYS offers no flush to disk, and the compression goes ahead without
it.  A compression never replaces a file that already has the \&.gz
name.  The \&.gz file gets the permission bits of the file that it
compresses, as gzip(1) gives them, and its owner and group under the
same rule as the new live file of a rotation.

When cfg->compress_rotated is true, the open finishes what an earlier
process left unfinished in the directory, for generations alone.  It
removes every <rotated-name>.gz.tmp file of path that passes the same
test of type, owner and timestamp.  A \&.gz file beside its own
uncompressed file is not known to be complete, while the uncompressed
file holds everything, so the open removes that \&.gz file.  It then
queues every uncompressed generation of path for compression, the
oldest first.  A generation whose \&.gz name holds an entry that is not
a generation stays uncompressed.

A process that ends with exit(3), or a return from main(), while the
logger is still open drains it within a budget of 5 seconds for the
whole process: an exit handler that the library registers when it
loads delivers what an async logger still holds, as a
clog_fatal(3) record does, and waits for the queued compressions.
The budget also bounds every lock that the drain takes: a logger whose
lock another thread holds past the budget is skipped, and the records
that it still holds can be lost.  Every write of the drain is bounded
too, on a pipe, a socket and a terminal as well; see
clog_open_fd_mp(3).  A compression that the budget cuts off stops,
removes its \&.tmp output and keeps the uncompressed file, and the next
open of path compresses it.  _exit(2), a signal and abort(3) run no
exit handler.

One file has one backing store in a process whenever a logger of it
rotates.  When a rotating logger of this process already writes the
file, or when this call rotates and a logger of this process already
writes the file, the call joins that logger, exactly as clog_derive(3)
does: the new handle has its own min_level and its own fields, and
shares the output target, the format, the rotation and the async
writer thread.  The file is the same when the directory and the last
component of path are the same, whatever spelling of the path each
call uses.  The call returns CLOG_INVALID instead when its rotation
configuration, its async configuration (NULL or not, and every
resolved value) or its allocator differs from the one of that logger,
and also when one of the two rotates and the other does not, because
two independent backing stores on one rotating file would each rename,
delete and compress files that the other one still writes, and lose
records.  Two opens of one file that both have rotation off stay two
independent loggers, each of which appends whole records to the file.
A call for a file whose last clog_close(3) still runs waits until that
close has finished.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
path       Destination log file path. Copied internally.
min_level  Messages below this level are silently dropped.
cfg        The rotation configuration, or NULL to turn rotation off.
           A max_file_size or max_rotated_files <= 0, and a
           rotation_interval_us of 0, fall back to their respective
           CLOG_DEFAULT_* constant:
             size_rotation_enabled  - turns size rotation on
             max_file_size          - int64_t bytes threshold
                                      (<= 0 = 10 MiB)
             time_rotation_enabled  - turns time rotation on
             rotation_interval_us   - uint64_t microseconds
                                      (0 = 86400000000 = 1 day)
             max_rotated_files      - files kept (<= 0 = 7)
             compress_rotated       - gzip-compress each rotated file
           max_file_size is a fixed-width int64_t and
           rotation_interval_us is a uint64_t, so this struct has one
           layout for each target, for all values of
           _FILE_OFFSET_BITS and _TIME_BITS in the including
           translation unit.  The caller needs no particular
           compilation flag for the struct to mean the same thing on
           both sides of the library boundary.
async_cfg  The configuration for async logging, or NULL for
           synchronous logging.  When it is not NULL, a log_*() call
           formats its own message alone and hands the rest to a
           dedicated writer thread.  See clog_open_fd_mp(3) for the
           full field list.  Unlike cfg above, a struct of all zeros
           still turns async mode on, with every zero field falling
           back to its own default; only a literal NULL keeps the
           logging synchronous.
mprocs     A custom allocator, which holds function pointers for
           malloc, free, calloc and realloc.  Pass NULL to use the
           default system allocator.  All four pointers must be set
           when mprocs is not NULL; a struct that is only part filled
           makes the call return CLOG_INVALID and creates no logger.
           The library copies the struct internally, so the caller
           can free it after this call.
           Every logger that clog_derive() makes from this one
           inherits the allocator.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a new logger handle on success.
Returns CLOG_INVALID after a failure: a bad path, a permission error,
an mprocs that is not valid, or a failed allocation; size rotation
that is on when the function cannot find the current size of a file
that already exists; a file that a rotating logger of this process
already writes with another configuration; or a rotating open of a
file that a logger of this process without rotation writes.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The logger owns the file descriptor and closes it on clog_close().
O_CLOEXEC is set on the file descriptor.
The library does the rename and the open of the new fd inside the
locked section, so no log line is lost, and no log line falls between
others across the rotation boundary.
When compress_rotated is true, the gzip compression runs on the
compression thread of the logger, after the new fd is already in use,
and outside the mutex, so the I/O of the compression stalls no log
writer.
The directory descriptor of a rotating logger carries O_CLOEXEC, as
the file descriptor does.
There are two supported ways to use fork(2) with clogger:
  - call fork(2) before you open a logger;
  - open a logger, call fork(2), and then call exec(3) immediately.
A process that continues with no exec(3) while a handle from before
the fork is live does not use a supported pattern.

As a defense in depth, a handle that is open at the time of such a
fork is technically usable in the child: log, derive and close
operations on that handle work correctly on both sides.  Because the
async writer thread of a logger does not exist in a forked child, an
inherited handle with async mode on changes to synchronous logging in
the child.  This change is transparent.

This fallback is part of the fork protection of the library, which a
build with -DCCOL_FORK_SAFETY_REQUIRED=0 does not include; such a
process must not fork while it holds a live async logger.

The compression thread does not exist in the child either.  The
compressions that the parent put in its queue stay with the parent,
and a later rotation in the child starts its own compression thread.

When async_cfg is not NULL, a batch that the writer thread builds can
cover several records that the application logged moments apart, and
such a batch can cross a rotation boundary.  The library writes it to
whichever file is current at the time of the flush, instead of
splitting the batch exactly at the moment at which each record was
logged.

The kernel can accept only part of a write, for example when a
filesystem has just run out of space or an RLIMIT_FSIZE ceiling is
reached.  The bytes that it did take then sit on the disk with no
terminating newline for that record.  A synchronous logger writes that
newline itself, so the next record starts a line of its own and
nothing joins it onto the fragment; the logger then writes a "log
record truncated: <n> of <m> bytes written" record that names the
loss.  An async logger keeps the bytes that the write did not take in
its batching buffer and offers them again on the next flush.  Because
they are the exact continuation of what did reach the disk, the logger
completes the record and delivers every record behind it once the
condition clears.  Both size rotation and time rotation stand down
while those bytes are outstanding, so the two halves of a record
always reach the same file.  The short write makes a rotation due, and
the flush that finally delivers those bytes takes it; until then the
current file can sit past cfg->max_file_size by the rest of one batch.

A flush keeps the undelivered bytes only after an error that a later
attempt can outlast: ENOSPC, EDQUOT, EFBIG, ENOBUFS, ENOMEM, or EAGAIN
or EINTR that ends a wait.  Any other write error, such as EPIPE,
ECONNRESET or EBADF, fails the same way on every retry, so the flush
drops the undelivered bytes and writes a "log record truncated:
undelivered batch dropped (records=<n> bytes=<m>): write error <errno>"
record in their place, and the records after it go out as usual.  The
last flush of clog_close(3) has no later retry, so what it cannot
deliver is named the same way, with "the logger closed" as the reason.

Some records have to reach the file before those bytes do, and the
library never writes such a record between the two halves of the
record that those bytes belong to.  There are two such cases: a
CLOG_FMT_SYSLOG record, which the library never batches, and a record
that takes the synchronous write path.  Each one first gives the
outstanding bytes one more attempt at delivery of its own.  When the
file still will not take them, it gives up on them and on every
remaining line of the split record, backtrace lines included, and then
writes the missing newline of the split record (when a line is open)
and a "log record truncated: <n> undelivered bytes dropped" record in
their place.  A logfmt record whose backtrace lines are still
outstanding counts as split even when the bytes already on the disk
end with the newline of its first line.  The library keeps the records
that the batch holds behind the split one, and a later flush delivers
them, so they follow the record that it had to write past them.
Giving up on a continuation leaves nothing outstanding, so the next
check takes the rotation that stood down for it, exactly as it would
after a delivery.

A write to a regular file cannot raise SIGPIPE, and a logger on one
never touches the disposition of SIGPIPE or any signal mask.  When
path names a FIFO, the logger treats it as a pipe; see
clog_set_sigpipe_policy(3).
.fi
